Host-side gtest unit tests for crosspoint-reader.

Build and run everything with the bin/run-tests wrapper (fork-only; macOS and
Linux; falls back to the CMake/Ninja copies PlatformIO bundles when they are
not on PATH):

  bin/run-tests

  bin/run-tests --asan            # AddressSanitizer + UBSan, into build/test-asan
  bin/run-tests --filter css      # only suites matching the regex (dir or target)
  bin/run-tests --quick           # skip the two slowest suites (pre-push hook)
  bin/run-tests --full            # also run pio check afterwards
  bin/run-tests --clean           # wipe the build directory first

CROSSPOINT_TEST_BUILD_DIR overrides the build directory and
CROSSPOINT_TEST_JOBS caps build parallelism when several checkouts or agents
build at the same time.

The same full run is also a PlatformIO target (registered by
scripts/register_unit_tests_target.py):

  pio run -t unit-tests

Or drive CMake directly:

  cmake -S test -B build/test
  cmake --build build/test
  ctest --test-dir build/test --output-on-failure -j

Run a single suite directly:

  cmake --build build/test --target StreamingJsonParserTest
  build/test/streaming_json_parser/StreamingJsonParserTest --gtest_filter='*'

Google Test is fetched via CMake FetchContent on first configure; the pinned
version lives in test/CMakeLists.txt.

Layout
------

One directory per suite. Each has its own CMakeLists.txt with a single
add_executable(<Name>Test ...) that compiles the production sources under test
directly, plus a stubs/ folder where the hardware layer has to be faked.
test/CMakeLists.txt registers each with one crosspoint_suite(<dir>) line; that
function honours CROSSPOINT_SUITE_DIRS so a --filter or --quick run configures
only the named directories and an unrelated in-progress suite cannot break it.
bin/run-tests discovers suites by scanning the per-suite CMakeLists.txt files,
so adding a directory plus its crosspoint_suite() line is all that is needed.

Shared pieces:

  test/support/       host stand-ins, PlatformHost and AllocCounter (fork-only)
  test/corpus/        committed malformed-input corpus, see its README (fork-only)
  test/*_common/      per-format helpers and stubs shared by related suites
                      (dict_common, fb2_common, image_common, kosync_common)
  test/epubs/, test/fb2/, ...   committed fixtures, regenerated by
                      scripts/generate_test_*.py (FB2:
                      scripts/generate_fb2_test_fixtures.py)

crosspoint_suite(), CROSSPOINT_SANITIZE and CROSSPOINT_SUITE_DIRS in
test/CMakeLists.txt are all fork-only -- upstream lists its suites with plain
add_subdirectory(). test/CMakeLists.txt itself and `pio run -t unit-tests`
exist upstream.

Suites that parse XML compile the in-tree expat (lib/expat) with the firmware's
flags, -DXML_GE=0 and -DXML_CONTEXT_BYTES=1024, rather than the host system's
expat, so host behavior matches the device.

CI runs the test program on every pull request; the second, sanitized leg
(-DCROSSPOINT_SANITIZE=ON) is fork-only.
